# Writing Effective Skills

**Trigger**: Creating, editing, or reviewing a SKILL.md.

## Core Principle

A skill is context engineering, not documentation. Every sentence must change what the model does. If removing it would not change behavior, delete it.

**The test**: Would the agent get this wrong without this instruction? If no, the content cannot afford to be there.

## When to Create a Skill

**Create when**: The agent gets it wrong without special context; domain expertise/taste matters; workflow consistency is critical.

**Do NOT create when**: The model already knows the workflow (e.g., `git log`); it recapitulates `.cursor/rules/` content; the knowledge changes faster than you can maintain it.

## Description (Routing Trigger)

The description determines whether the skill loads. A skill that does not trigger reliably is broken.

- Write in third person
- Dense with trigger terms — pack keywords the agent will match
- Include WHAT and WHEN
- Target <50 words
- Test against sibling skills to avoid semantic overlap

**Bad**: `description: Helps with PDFs`

**Good**: `description: Extract text and tables from PDF files, fill forms, merge documents. Use when working with PDF files or when the user mentions PDFs, forms, or document extraction.`

## SKILL.md Body Order

Models follow a U-shaped attention curve — best at the beginning and end, worst in the middle. Order sections accordingly:

| Section | Position |
|---------|----------|
| Frontmatter | Top |
| Opening contract | Top |
| Defaults / inputs | Top |
| Required artifacts | Top |
| Global constraints | Top |
| Workflow phases | Middle |
| Human checkpoints | Middle-late |
| Guardrails / gotchas | End |
| Final handoff | End |

Never bury a hard constraint in the middle of workflow phases.

## Conciseness

- Target 150-300 lines, hard cap 500 lines
- Use progressive disclosure: put heavy content in `references/`, `scripts/`, or `assets/`
- Keep file references one level deep from SKILL.md

## One Skill, One Workflow

Split skills when they have materially different triggers, outputs, or decision rules. Avoid "if mode X / if mode Y" sections. Accept structural duplication between similar skills until at least three consumers exist.

## Scripts

Use for parsing, validation, formatting — mechanical tasks easier to run than describe. Never hide core judgment or routing logic inside scripts. Every script must specify: when to run it, inputs, and what outputs count as success. Never require interactive input.

## Human Checkpoints

Name the exact trigger that requires human input. "Ask if you're unsure" adds constraint load on every action and leads to always-asking or never-asking.

**Good**: "Ask before creating a missing memory file."
**Bad**: "Ask if you're unsure."

## Anti-Patterns

- Vague description — weak routing signal
- Multi-mode skill with conditional branches — models fail at nested constraint composition
- Critical rule buried late — later instructions are more likely to be dropped
- Laundry list of edge cases in SKILL.md — bloats context, dilutes core instructions
- Interactive scripts — hangs in autonomous runs
- Core behavior hidden in companion files — breaks on clients that only load SKILL.md
- Windows-style paths (`scripts\helper.py`) — breaks cross-platform
- Time-sensitive info — becomes outdated
- Blanket "ask when uncertain" — adds constraint load on every action

**Good example**: `.cursor/skills/over-engineering-review/SKILL.md` — single workflow (complexity review), dense trigger terms ("over-engineering", "delete", "simplify"), output format specified (one line per finding), scope boundaries clear (complexity only, not correctness/security).

## Security

Skills influence planning, tool usage, and command execution. Treat as privileged instructions. Never assume network access or elevated permissions. Require explicit approval for sensitive actions.

## Verification Checklist

- [ ] Description says WHAT and WHEN in <50 words, third person
- [ ] SKILL.md body under 500 lines
- [ ] One skill, one workflow — no conditional branches
- [ ] Critical constraints at top, guardrails at end
- [ ] No content the model already knows
- [ ] Progressive disclosure for heavy content
- [ ] File references one level deep
- [ ] Scripts non-interactive with documented I/O
- [ ] Human checkpoints concrete and sparse
- [ ] No time-sensitive information
